            <ArticleTitle>A public health outbreak management framework applied to surges in opioid overdoses.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>273-281</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">jom.2017.0396</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5055/jom.2017.0396</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Over the past decade, Canada and the United States have been facing an epidemic of harms from prescription opioids. More recently, opioid-naïve individuals have been exposed to illicit opioids through adulterated combination products. This has resulted in sudden surges of opioid-related mortality. A proactive public health solution is needed to prevent further death. We propose examining these surges in opioid overdoses as outbreaks and investigating them in a similar way to an outbreak of an infectious disease. An epidemiologic investigation model for opioid overdose outbreaks, that could be modified by other public health agencies, is discussed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
